      "id": "01",
      "title": "What is a data race?",
      "question": "What exactly makes two accesses a data race?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "ThreadSanitizer, question one. What exactly makes two memory accesses a data race? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What exactly makes two accesses a <em>data race</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "A data race needs three things. Two threads touch the same location, and at least one of them writes. And nothing orders the two accesses. Ordering means happens before. Within a thread, program order chains each step to the next. Across threads, an unlock synchronizes with the next lock of the same mutex. So there's a path from the write to the read, and no race. Remove the mutex, and the path is gone. That's a data race, which in C++ is undefined behavior: the compiler may assume it never happens, and, for example, hoist a flag load out of a loop.",
          "props": {
            "title": "No path, <em>no order</em>",
            "nodes": [
