    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Vector clocks",
      "question": "How does TSan decide that two accesses are unordered?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. At runtime, how does ThreadSanitizer decide that two accesses were not ordered? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "How does TSan decide two accesses are <em>unordered</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Each thread carries a vector clock: its own counter, plus the latest time it has heard from every other thread. Thread one writes x at its time four, and the shadow memory for x records: written by thread one, at four. On unlock, thread one leaves its clock in the mutex. When thread two locks, it merges that clock, taking the maximum of each entry, so it now knows thread one's time four. Thread two reads x. The check: is the last write's time, four, at most what thread two knows of thread one? Yes, so the write happens before the read. Without the lock, thread two's entry for thread one is still zero. Four is greater than zero, so the accesses are concurrent, and ThreadSanitizer reports a race, with both stacks.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Did I <em>hear</em> about that write?",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "w",
                "label": "T1: x = 42",
                "x": 300,
                "y": 290,
                "w": 250,
                "h": 64
              },
