    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Using it",
      "question": "What does TSan report beyond races, and what are its limits?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. What does ThreadSanitizer report besides data races, and what are its limits? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What does TSan report beyond races, and what are its <em>limits</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Build with the thread sanitizer flag, plus debug info, and some optimization. A plain bool used as a stop flag is the classic race: the report shows the write, the previous read, and where each thread was created. Make it atomic. It also tracks the order in which mutexes are taken. One thread takes m1 then m2, another takes m2 then m1. Even if this run never deadlocks, it reports a lock order inversion, a potential deadlock.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Races, and <em>potential</em> deadlocks",
            "file": "app.cpp",
            "tag": "-fsanitize=thread",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 20,
            "width": 1190,
            "code": "// clang++ -fsanitize=thread -g -O1 app.cpp && ./a.out\nbool done = false;                    // should be std::atomic<bool>\n\nvoid worker()     { while (!done) step(); }        // read\nvoid controller() { sleep_for(1s); done = true; }  // write: races with the read\n\nstd::mutex m1, m2;\nvoid a() { std::lock_guard l1(m1); std::lock_guard l2(m2); }  // m1 → m2\nvoid b() { std::lock_guard l2(m2); std::lock_guard l1(m1); }  // m2 → m1\n\n// WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race\n//   Write of size 1 by thread T2: controller() app.cpp:5\n//   Previous read of size 1 by thread T1: worker() app.cpp:4\n// WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: lock-order-inversion (potential deadlock)",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "flag",
                "lines": "1",
                "note": "Instrument every access + sync op"
              },
              {
                "at": "classic",
                "lines": "2-5",
                "note": "Plain bool shared across threads"
              },
              {
                "at": "report",
                "lines": "11-13",
                "note": "Both stacks: the write and the earlier read"
              },
              {
                "at": "order",
                "lines": "8-9",
                "note": "m1→m2 here, m2→m1 there"
              },
              {
                "at": "inversion",
                "lines": "14",
                "note": "Reported even if this run didn't hang"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Its limits. It only sees races on paths that actually ran, with real interleavings. Each eight bytes keep only four shadow cells, so an old access can be evicted, and a race missed. Code it didn't compile, like inline assembly or an uninstrumented library, is invisible. And it costs five to fifteen times the CPU, and five to ten times the memory.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Where TSan <em>goes blind</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "ran",
                "label": "Dynamic",
                "title": "Only executed interleavings",
                "body": "Stress tests help; no proof of absence.",
                "code": "run it in CI"
              },
              {
                "at": "evicted",
                "label": "History",
                "title": "4 shadow cells per 8 bytes",
                "body": "Old accesses get evicted.",
                "code": "history_size"
              },
              {
                "at": "compile",
                "label": "Coverage",
                "title": "Uninstrumented code",
                "body": "Inline asm, prebuilt libraries.",
                "code": "rebuild deps"
              },
              {
                "at": "fifteen",
                "label": "Cost",
                "title": "5–15× CPU",
                "body": "5–10× memory; never in the same build as ASan.",
                "code": "-fsanitize=thread"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. A data race is two accesses, one a write, with no happens before between them. ThreadSanitizer tracks that with vector clocks and shadow memory. It also catches lock order inversions. And it only sees what your tests actually run.",
          "props": {
            "title": "TSan, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "race",
                "text": "Race = same location, a write, no happens-before.",
                "code": "UB in C++"
              },
              {
                "at": "clocks",
                "text": "Vector clocks + shadow cells decide order.",
                "code": "release → acquire"
              },
              {
                "at": "inversions",
                "text": "Also flags lock-order inversions.",
                "code": "potential deadlock"
              },
              {
                "at": "sees",
                "text": "Sees only executed paths: keep it in CI.",
                "code": "-fsanitize=thread"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
